## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests candidates on practical lock-free allocator design with emphasis on per-core caching, ABA avoidance, and NUMA-aware padding. Also probes understanding of microbenchmarking for contention analysis.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free memory allocation and per-core caches
- ABA avoidance with tagged pointers or version counters
- Cache-line alignment and padding to prevent false sharing
- NUMA-aware data placement and cross-core contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct Block {
    struct Block* next;
    uintptr_t tag;
    unsigned char data[128];
} Block;

static _Atomic(Block*) g_free_list = NULL;

static Block* fetch_chunk(void) {
    Block* chunk = aligned_alloc(64, 256 * sizeof(Block));
    for (int i = 0; i < 255; i++) {
        chunk[i].next = &chunk[i + 1];
        chunk[i].tag = 0;
    }
    chunk[255].next = NULL;
    chunk[255].tag = 0;
    return chunk;
}

static Block* alloc_block(void) {
    Block* b = local_cache;  // thread-local
    do {
        if (b) return b;
    } while ((b = atomic_load_explicit(&g_free_list, memory_order_acquire)) 
             && !atomic_compare_exchange_weak(&g_free_list, &b, b->next));
    return b ? b : fetch_chunk();
}
```

## Microbenchmark Plan
1. **Baseline**: Measure allocation latency with warm local cache
2. **Contention test**: Scale threads from 8 to 32, measure global pool miss rate
3. **NUMA sensitivity**: Pin threads to cores on different sockets, measure cross-socket access overhead
4. **Metrics**: Track local-cache hits, global-pool fetches, CAS retry rate, and allocation/free latency percentiles

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives, correctness under contention, and memory ordering pitfalls in a beginner-friendly yet non-trivial way.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic operations and memory orders
- FIFO fairness and blocking behavior
- Spinlock design trade-offs and wrap-around handling
- Basic micro-benchmarking considerations under contention

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct {
  atomic_uint64_t next_ticket;
  atomic_uint64_t now_serving;
} ticket_lock_t;

void ticket_lock_acquire(ticket_lock_t *lock) {
  uint64_t my = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lock->next_ticket, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&lock->now_serving, memory_order_acquire) != my) {
    // busy-wait
  }
}

void ticket_lock_release(ticket_lock_t *lock) {
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lock->now_serving, 1, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for fairness under CPU migrations on a NUMA system?
- What are practical mitigations to reduce bus traffic on high contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of safe, non-blocking reclamation in a shared object graph, balancing hazard pointers with epoch-based techniques. The scenario emphasizes NUMA locality and cross-thread safety, common in database-like systems.

## Key Concepts

- Hazard pointers
- Epoch-based reclamation
- Per-thread fast paths
- Memory ordering and fences
- NUMA considerations

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct Obj {
  atomic_uint refcount;
  atomic_uint epoch;
  struct Obj* next;
} Obj;

void pin(Obj* o) { atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&o->refcount, 1, memory_order_acquire); }
void unpin(Obj* o) { if (atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&o->refcount, 1, memory_order_release) == 1) retire(o); }

void retire(Obj* o) { add_to_retire_list(o, current_thread); if (should_advance_epoch()) scan_and_free(o); }
void* get(Obj* o) { pin(o); /* access */ }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle long-lived objects and cycles with epoch-based reclamation?
- What are the edge cases when a thread exits or stalls while objects are pinned?

